00:10:07,420 --> 00:10:08,400
It's just one quick action.

281
00:10:08,600 --> 00:10:09,820
He takes it and passes it.

282
00:10:10,200 --> 00:10:10,920
And the action is finished.

283
00:10:11,360 --> 00:10:12,360
Yes.

284
00:10:12,360 --> 00:10:14,900
I think we can see a few more examples of this.

285
00:10:16,120 --> 00:10:16,780
Let's listen again.

286
00:10:18,720 --> 00:10:21,950
The Russians win possession and immediately set up their attack.

287
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:28,260
The Russians win possession and immediately set up their attack.

288
00:10:30,350 --> 00:10:34,770
Here, we can hear that he is using the present simple again.

289
00:10:35,720 --> 00:10:38,750
Because these are very short actions that finish quickly.

290
00:10:39,120 --> 00:10:40,120
Right?

291
00:10:40,120 --> 00:10:41,120
Exactly.

292
00:10:41,120 --> 00:10:50,680
And it's actually very, very common when you're describing something you see that's happening now, that's really exciting.

293
00:10:50,680 --> 00:10:52,210
It's common to use present simple.

294
00:10:52,410 --> 00:10:52,690
Exactly.

295
00:10:53,070 --> 00:10:56,810
If you pay attention, most sporting events are in the same way.

296
00:10:57,730 --> 00:11:01,530
They would describe the actions that are happening in the present simple.

297
00:11:01,530 --> 00:11:02,590
Not present progressive.

298
00:11:03,000 --> 00:11:04,000
Yeah.

